id: pm-ki67py
title: "The one-release-per-day guard compares a prefix glob against unpadded date keys, so it is correct only by accident of tag creation order and silently skips a real release for any out-of-order tag"
description: "The one-release-per-day guarantee is enforced by a string prefix glob over unpadded date keys, so the check does not actually test \"was a release cut today\". It currently gives the right answer for a reason unrelated to its logic, and this project has already lost a release once to this exact class.\n\nMechanism, read at HEAD:\n\n- utcDateKey (scripts/release/utils.mjs:67) builds the key from getUTCMonth()+1 and getUTCDate() with NO zero padding, so 1 May is 2026.5.1, not 2026.05.01.\n- listTodayTags (scripts/release/run-release-pipeline.mjs:88) runs git tag --list with the pattern v${todayKey}* and treats ANY match as proof that today already has a release.\n- maybeSkipForSameDayRelease (:263) then skips the entire pipeline and reports reason release_already_cut_today together with a tags_today list.\n\nThe glob is prefix-unsafe for every single-digit day whose number is a prefix of a two-digit day in the same month. Probing the 78 real tags in this repository, 68 derivable day-keys produce 45 cross-day matches: the key 2026.5.1 glob-matches v2026.5.10, v2026.5.11, v2026.5.12, v2026.5.14 and v2026.5.18; 2026.5.2 matches v2026.5.23, .24, .27, .28, .29; 2026.6.1 matches v2026.6.10 through v2026.6.19; and so on. Days 1 to 9 of every month are affected, which is roughly 30 percent of the calendar.\n\nHonest statement of current exposure: under strictly forward-moving time with tags only ever created for the current day, the colliding tags do not exist yet at the moment the guard runs, so no false skip has occurred and today's behaviour is correct - the 2026-07-28 guard matched exactly v2026.7.28. The defect is that this correctness is incidental. The check passes today because of the order in which tags happen to be created, not because the comparison is right.\n\nIt becomes a real skipped release the moment any tag exists out of chronological order: a re-cut or re-tag of an earlier day, a hotfix tagged against a prior date, an imported or mirrored tag set, a fork or a clone whose tags arrive in bulk from a fetch, or any repair of the seven tags this project has already declared and never delivered. In each of those cases the guard silently reports release_already_cut_today for a day that has no release, and the pipeline exits reporting ok:true and skipped:true, which is indistinguishable from a healthy no-op.\n\nPrecedent that this class is not theoretical here: a padded-versus-unpadded version match previously caused silently skipped releases in this project. The fix then was applied to the matching site that failed. The date-key glob is the same mistake in the sibling site and was not covered.\n\nThe correct check is an exact comparison against a parsed tag's own date fields, or a zero-padded canonical key, rather than a prefix glob. The skip decision is also the right place for a negative-control fixture, since a guard that can only be observed skipping correctly is a guard nobody can prove wrong.\n\nScope is the same-day comparison only. The drifting cron window has its own owner, the scheduled-run failure rate has its own owner, and the three-way divergence between changelog, tags and npm has its own owner. This is none of those: it is whether the comparison that enforces one-release-per-day is testing what it claims."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[3]: ci,core,release
created_at: "2026-07-28T02:58:02.043Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-03T06:33:42.245Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-03T06:24:32.173Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-03T06:24:32.173Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 150
acceptance_criteria: "The same-day release check compares parsed date fields or a zero-padded canonical key rather than a prefix glob, so no tag from a different day can ever satisfy it; A negative-control fixture proves the guard skips when a same-day tag exists and does NOT skip when only a later-day tag from the same month exists; Days 1 through 9 of a month are covered explicitly by regression tests, including the case where a two-digit-day tag from the same month already exists; The skip envelope's tags_today list contains only tags whose own date equals the run's date key; The unpadded date key is either padded canonically or documented as intentional with every consumer audited for prefix-matching"
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: 93
resolution: "listTodayTags now parses each tag's own calendar fields and compares them exactly to the target date key, replacing the v${todayKey}* prefix glob; parseReleaseTagDate is exported and negative-control tested."
expected_result: The one-release-per-day guard reports a same-day release only when a tag for that exact calendar day exists.
actual_result: "Confirmed against the repository's 78 real tags: the glob produced 7 spurious same-day matches for 2026.5.1 and 7 for 2026.6.1; exact comparison returns only the genuine same-day tags and returns empty for a day with no release. 34 tests pass."
