comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-01T09:53:04.517Z","harness:claude-code","Guard re-confirmed live 2026-08-01 at HEAD, with today's date being the case that makes it visible. listTodayTags (scripts/release/run-release-pipeline.mjs:88-89) runs git tag --list \"v${todayKey}*\" where todayKey comes from utcDateKey (scripts/release/utils.mjs:80-82) and is deliberately unpadded: today it is 2026.8.1, so the glob is v2026.8.1*, which matches v2026.8.1 and would equally match v2026.8.10 through v2026.8.19. The 82 tags in this repository are all v20*-prefixed and were created in chronological order, so no future-dated tag exists to be matched -- which is precisely the accident the item names. The .github/workflows/auto-release.yml same-day retry path at line 163 uses an exact git tag --list \"v${today_version}\" with no glob, so the two same-day checks in the release path disagree about their own matching rule: one exact, one prefix. Today's observed behaviour was correct: three Auto Release runs (workflow_dispatch 02:23 which published, workflow_dispatch 02:51, schedule 05:41), exactly one tag v2026.8.1, one GitHub release created 02:44:13Z, and npm dist-tags.latest 2026.8.1 modified 02:43:26Z. One release for the day, as intended, by luck of ordering rather than by the guard's construction."
  "2026-08-03T06:24:31.365Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence, 2026-08-03. Replaced the prefix glob in listTodayTags with exact comparison on parsed tag calendar fields, and exported parseReleaseTagDate so the tag grammar is testable on its own.\n\nVerified against the real 78-tag set in this repository. Old behaviour vs new, same input:\n  key 2026.5.1  glob returned v2026.5.1 v2026.5.1-2 v2026.5.10 v2026.5.11 v2026.5.12 v2026.5.14 v2026.5.18 -> exact returns v2026.5.1 v2026.5.1-2\n  key 2026.6.1  glob returned v2026.6.1 v2026.6.10 v2026.6.11 v2026.6.12 v2026.6.13 v2026.6.16 v2026.6.17 v2026.6.19 -> exact returns v2026.6.1\n  key 2026.8.3  glob returned v2026.8.3 -> exact returns v2026.8.3 (unchanged, today is not a prefix-colliding day)\n  key 2026.8.4  exact returns [] (a day with no release reports no release)\n\nNegative-control coverage added, which is the part the original guard could not have: the fixture contains v2026.6.1, v2026.6.1-2, v2026.6.10, v2026.6.12, v2026.6.19, v2026.6.2 and asserts that 2026.6.1 matches only its own two tags, that 2026.6.11 matches nothing despite five prefix neighbours, and that non-release tags (v2026.6.1-rc1, pm-cli-v2026.6.1, nightly) never parse as a release date. An invalid date key now fails loudly instead of globbing.\n\nnode scripts/run-tests.mjs test -- tests/unit/scripts/release/run-release-pipeline.spec.ts: 34 passed."
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-30T09:21:21.674Z","harness:claude-code","First day the collision operand pair actually exists in the tag set. git tag --list \"v2026.7.3*\" now returns TWO tags: v2026.7.3 (July 3) and v2026.7.30 (July 30). The unpadded utcDateKey plus the v-prefix glob is therefore no longer a hypothetical pairing - the corpus now contains a real tag whose name is a strict prefix of another real tag in the same series.\n\nThe honest scope of this item still holds and must not be widened: on 2026-07-03 the key was 2026.7.3 and v2026.7.30 did not yet exist, so the glob could not have matched it; on 2026-07-30 the key is 2026.7.30 and the glob matches only itself. The guard remains correct by accident of forward-moving time, not by logic. What changed today is that the reachability argument no longer needs a hypothetical tag - it needs only a clock that is not monotonic (a re-run against an older ref, a backfilled tag, or a manual dispatch keyed to a past date)."
  "2026-07-31T19:41:25.247Z","harness:claude-code","Sequencing edge recorded 2026-07-31: this defect is now blocked_by pm-uenmu9 rather than merely adjacent to it.\n\nThe reason is that the correct fix is not determined by the defect. The guard compares a prefix glob against unpadded date keys, so it is wrong in a way that has two different correct repairs depending on a policy question nobody has answered: if one release per UTC day is an architectural invariant, the guard should be an exact-match assertion that refuses a second same-day version outright; if a manually triggered same-day release is a supported operation, the guard must distinguish scheduled from dispatched invocations and permit the latter while still refusing an accidental duplicate from the former. Those are different comparisons over different key spaces, and implementing either one before the ruling means implementing it twice.\n\nThe workflow already contains a partial answer that the ADR has not ratified. The issues-triggered retry path in .github/workflows/auto-release.yml computes an unpadded today_version, checks git tag --list for an exact v<version> match, and on a hit reruns the existing tag's Release workflow instead of creating a replacement version - with an inline comment stating it never creates a replacement same-day version. So the retry path behaves as though one-per-day is architectural, while the scheduled path's guard lives in run-release-pipeline.mjs and is the one this defect describes. Two paths, one undocumented assumption, no ruling.\n\nThis edge makes that dependency traversable instead of leaving it as prose in two separate items."
